## ABSTRACT
The urgent expectation of the next-generation energy storage devices for electric vehicles has driven researchers’ attention to the lithium-oxygen $(\mathsf{L i}.\mathsf{O}_{2})$ batteries due to the satisfied specific energy density. Herein, spatially-controlled $\mathsf{C o}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ nanoflake arrays with three-dimensionalnetworked morphology are adopted as flexible and self-standing oxygen cathodes in $L i{\cdot}O_{2}$ batteries. The spinel-phase $\mathsf{C o}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ nanoflakes were converted from two-dimension metal-organic frameworks with abundant available channels and large specific surface area. The open-structure nanoflake arrays possess sufficient $\mathsf{L i}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{2}/$ cathode contact interface, great bifunctional catalytic performance and adequate $\mathsf{L i}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{2}$ accommodation, leading to the enhanced electrochemical performance of the $L i{\cdot}O_{2}$ batteries. As expected, the binder-free porous $\mathsf{C o}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}/\mathsf{C T}$ cathode delivers a high capacity of $6{,}509\mathrm{\mAh{\cdot}g}^{-1}(200\mathrm{\mA{\cdot}g}^{-1})$ and enhanced stability over 100 cycles (limited by $1,000\mathsf{m A h{\cdot g}}^{-1})$ . In addition, pouch-type $L i{\mathrm{-}}O_{2}$ batteries were successfully designed and cycled with $\mathsf{C o}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}/\mathsf{C T}$ cathode as oxygen electrodes, demonstrating its potential application for flexible electronics and wearable energy storage devices.
## KEYWORDS
two-dimension metal-organic frameworks, mesoporous $\mathsf{C o}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ , flexible oxygen electrode, lithium-oxygen batteries
## Introduction
The increased consumption of the traditional fossil fuels and the consequent severe environment pollution have forced global researchers to seek the renewable energy and energy storage equipment [1–7]. Up to date, lithium ion batteries have already been applied in the electric vehicles and hybrid electric vehicles with high working voltage and long-time stability [8]. However, due to the limited specific energy density, most of current electric vehicles on sale cannot yet challenge the 300-mile range of the average internal combustion engine [9–11]. Lithium-oxygen batteries have received much attention, for that the theoretical energy density $(3,505\mathrm{~Wh{\cdot}k g^{-1}})$ ) is much higher than the current state-of-the-art lithium ion batteries $(387~\mathrm{Wh{\cdot}k g^{-1}})$ ) [6, 7]. However, it should be noted that there are still many scientific challenges to be overcome before practical application of lithium-oxygen batteries, including the poor cycling stability, low rate capacity and instability of electrolyte [2, 3].
In the typical aprotic lithium-oxygen batteries, the principle is based on the chemical equation: $2\mathrm{Li}+\mathrm{O}_{2}\rightleftharpoons\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ ( $2.96\mathrm{~V~}$ vs. $\mathrm{Li/Li^{\circ}}$ . During the discharging process, the metal lithium anode is oxidized by pure $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ into $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ [8, 12–14]. To facilitate the formation of $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2},$ appropriate electrocatalysts are required to catalyse the reaction with $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ into $\mathrm{LiO}_{2}$ , and further to form the $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ . This process is known as the oxygen reduction reaction (ORR). Noted that the insoluble and poorly conductive discharge products will stack and cover the electrocatalysts, leading to the damped ORR performance and limited discharge capacity [15, 16]. On the other hand, during the charging process, the discharge products $\left(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\right)$ are electrochemically decomposed into metal lithium and $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ . However, as mentioned above, the stacked electrocatalysts also cannot efficiently promote the decomposition of $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , for the low electrochemical kinetics of 3-phase-interface [4, 17]. As a result, a high charging overpotential is needed to oxidize the $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ . It is noted that the non-aqueous electrolyte (e.g., tetraethylene glycol dimethyl ether) is instable in the high charging overpotential and many side reactions accompany with the decomposition of electrolytes [18–20].
Seeking bifunctional electrocatalysts with high catalytic activities of both ORR and OER has been the efficient way to reduce the overpotential and enhance the cycling stability in the Li- $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ batteries. So far, noble metals and these oxides are reported to exhibit favorable activities towards ORR and OER. For instance, the Ru, $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{IrO}_{2}$ are studied as the most active OER catalysts, while the platinumbased catalysts are regarded as the most efficient catalysts towards ORR [21–23]. In spite of their excellent catalytic activity, the scarcity and high price have blocked the practical application at massive scale. Thus, the development of economically low-cost electrocatalysts towards ORR and OER can import the application prospect of the Li- $O_{2}$ batteries.
In addition to exploring bifunctional catalysts, the structure of the oxygen electrode also plays an extraordinary effect on cycling performance of Li- $O_{2}$ batteries. As reported, the porous carbon and carbon-supported catalysts possess the high specific surface area and have been widely reported to have great catalytic activity towards both ORR and OER, especially the metal-organic framework (MOF) derived materials [24–28]. Due to high porosities and tunable functionalities, the MOF-derived materials have been successfully introduced as the oxygen cathodes in the Li- $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ batteries [29–32]. However, the most reported MOF-derived materials are carbonaceous matrix embedded or hybridized with metal oxides. The carbon can directly react with discharge product $(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2})$ , forming the $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{CO}_{3}$ . The accumulation of parasitic products (e.g., $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{CO}_{3}.$ ) would inevitably cause the severe polarization and rapid degradation of Li- $O_{2}$ batteries. What’s more, the organic binders like PTFE, PVDF also can be decomposed into LiOH and LiF. Consequently, to avoid those undesired side reactions, the carbon-free and binder-free cathodes are designed recently [33–35].
## 2 Experimental section
## 2.1 Preparation of Co-MOF on CT
The $\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $(0.58~\mathrm{g})$ and 2-methylimidazole $(1.3\:\mathrm{g})$ were dissolved in $40~\mathrm{mL}$ deionized water, respectively. After mixing, a piece of activated carbon textile $(2\mathrm{cm}\times2.5\mathrm{cm}$ , the front of the CT protected by glass slide) was immersed into the mixture solution. It is noted that the carbon textiles were treated by concentrated nitric acid at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $12\mathrm{{h}}$ . The Co-MOF/CT sample was obtained after aged for $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ . Finally, the sample was cleaned with water and vacuum dried overnight. The Co-MOF powder without carbon textile were also collected by centrifugation and dried overnight.
## 2.2 Preparation of Co3O4 on CT
The Co-MOF/CT sample was first treated in Ar at $500~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ and then calcined in the air at $350^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ with same heating rate of $2\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}{\cdot}\mathrm{min}^{-1}$ . The final products were named as $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/C T}$ and mass loading of the $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}}$ on the carbon textile is calculated to be
$0.15\mathrm{\mg}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ . The Co-MOF powder were treated in the similar manner. The black $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ powder was named as CONS.
## 2.3 Structural and morphological characterization
The loading content of the $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}}$ on the carbon textile was obtained by the Thermogravimetric (TG)/Differential Thermal Analysis (DTA) on a PerkinElmer TGA 7 instrument. The structure and morphological information of the $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/C T}$ samples were analyzed by X-ray powder diffraction (XRD, Bruker D8), field-emission scanning electron microscopy (FE-SEM, FEI Quanta 400), and high-resolution transmission electron microscopy (HR-TEM, Hitachi H-800). An ASAP-2010 surface area analyzer was adopted to determine $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ adsorption–desorption. The specific surface area of the samples was calculated by the Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) method and the pore size distributions were determined by the Barrett–Joyner– Halenda (BJH) method. The chemical compositions of the surfaces of the $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/C T}$ were examined by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS, Thermo VG scientific).
## 2.4 Electrochemical measurements
The rotating disk electrode (RDE) techniques were conducted to evaluate electrocatalytic activity of the as-prepared electrocatalysts for ORR in $0.1\mathrm{~M~}$ KOH aqueous solution, recorded by CHI760E. The $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/C T}$ samples were cut into disc with a diameter of $8\mathrm{mm}$ and directly utilized as the oxygen electrode in the Li- $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ batteries. The CONS powder catalysts were mixed with $10~\mathrm{\wt.\%}$ of polytetrafluoroethylene (PTFE) binders and $40\mathrm{\wt.\%}$ porous carbon (Super P). The mixture was pressed into slice and pasted on carbon paper. All the samples have been dried at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $12\mathrm{{h}}$ . The electrochemical performance of the batteries was measured by using the 2032-type coin cells (MTI Corporation), where the Li foil works as the counter electrode. The electrolyte containing $1\mathbf{M}$ bis(trifluoromethane)sulfonimide lithium salt (LiTFSI) in tetraethylene glycol dimethyl ether (TEGDME) was dehydrated before usage and glass fiber membrane (Whatman, GF/D) was used to separate the metal lithium and oxygen cathode. The pouch-type cells were composed of $\mathrm{{Co_{3}O_{4}/C T}}$ samples as oxygen cathode, glass fiber membrane, lithium and electrolyte. The pouch-type cells were tested in a homemade container. The batteries were assembled in the glovebox under argon atmosphere (water $<1$ ppm and oxygen $<1~\mathrm{ppm}\rangle$ ). After assembled, the batteries were kept in the sealed containers full of high purity oxygen $(99.999\%)$ ). After stewing, galvanostatic charge/discharge performance was recorded by the CT2001A LAND Battery Test System (Wuhan, China) and the current density is normalized to weight of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ . The cycle voltammetry (CV) was conducted on the electrochemical workstation (CH Instruments, Inc. CHI760E). The electrochemical AC impedance spectroscopy (EIS) was obtained by electrochemical workstation under open-circuit voltage in the frequency range of $10^{5}\mathrm{Hz}$ to $10^{-2}$ Hz with an AC amplitude of $10~\mathrm{mV}$ .
## 3 Results and discussion
The synthesis process of the porous $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}}$ nanoflakes on the carbon textile is illustrated in the Fig. 1. The carbon textile was treated by concentrated nitric acid to improve its hydrophilicity. Then the acid-activated carbon textile was immersed in the aqueous mixture containing ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ and 2-methylimidazole at room temperature. The acid-activated carbon textile can adsorb the cobalt ions and 2-methylimidazole, which then coordinate and form the Co-MOF on the surface. It is noted that the Co-MOF can grow into leaf-like nanoflakes without the carbon textile (Fig. S1 in the ESM) [38]. The obtained Co-MOF/CT sample was then treated in the Ar at $500^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to maintain the morphology and then annealed in the air to gain the spinel-phase $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanoflakes. According to the TG analysis in
Fig. S2 in the ESM, the mass loading of the $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ on the carbon textile is calculated to be $5.5\mathrm{wt.\%}$ .
The crystallinity of the as-prepared sample was investigated by XRD in the Fig. 2(a). All the diffraction peaks of the $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/C T}$ can be assigned to the spinel-phase $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ (JCPDS $\#42\substack{-1467}$ ). The broad diffraction peaks at about $26^{\circ}$ and $43^{\circ}$ are attributed to the carbon textile. The broad peak width and low intensity of the spinel-phase $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ indicates that the as-prepared material is likely composed of nanocrystallines. The XPS measurements were conducted to analyse the surface composition of the as-prepared $\mathrm{{Co_{3}O_{4}/C T}}$ sample as shown in Figs. 2(b)–2(d). Two main peaks of $\mathrm{Co}~2\mathrm{p}$ spectra are located at 795.1 and $780.2\ \mathrm{eV},$ in accordance with the Co $2\mathsf{p}_{1/2}$ and Co $2{\tt p}_{3/2}$ spin-orbit peaks of $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}}$ [41–43]. After deconvolution, the fitting peaks of the ${\mathrm{Co}}^{3+}$ are located at 795.1 and $779.6\mathrm{eV},$ while fitting peaks at 796.8 and $781.2\ \mathrm{~eV}$ are assigned to the ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ , respectively [43, 44]. The O 1s XPS peak at $530.1\mathrm{eV}$ further confirms the formation of the $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4},$ which accords with the oxygen species in the $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ phase [42]. Based on above analysis, it can be revealed that the self-standing and binder-free $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/C T}$ composite is successfully obtained.
The morphology of the $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/C T}$ is further investigated by scanning electron microscopy (SEM). As shown in Figs. 3(a) and 3(b), the carbon textile is uniformly covered with the 2D Co-MOF. The further enlarged image of the Co-MOF indicates solid nature of these Co-based nanowalls with smooth surfaces in Fig. 3(b). The Co-MOF/CT samples were then thermally treated and Figs. 3(c) and 3(d) shows the typical of the converted $\mathrm{{Co_{3}O_{4}/C T}}$ samples. It can be observed that all the Co-MOF nanoflakes transform to the $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}}$ without pulverization and thus nanoarrays are completely maintained. What’s more, there are plenty of macropores and mesopores in the $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}}$ nanoflakes (insert graph), which are generated due to the release of $_\mathrm{H_{2}O}$ , $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and other small molecules during the thermal decomposition [45, 46]. The $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanoflakes connected with each other can improve electronic conductivity of electrode material, forming a highly open and porous structure. Besides, the porous structure with high surface area can also facilitate oxygen diffusion, benefit the infiltration of electrolyte and provide enough space for discharge products. For comparison, the Co-MOF without immersion of carbon textile was also prepared. The Co-MOF presents leaf-shaped morphology and delivers uneven size in Fig. S1 in the ESM. After heat treatment, most $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}}$ nanoflakes cannot maintain the leaf-shaped morphology and become fragment as shown in Fig. S3 in the ESM. In the paper, the acid-treated carbon textile is served as the growth substrate, which can well adjust the size of the Co-MOF to be uniform and maintain the morphology after calcining.
The HR-TEM images clearly show the typical morphology of the $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}}$ nanoflakes. As shown in Figs. 4(a)–4(c), the $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}}$ nanoflakes demonstrate the porous structure, consistent with the SEM results. The nanoflakes are constituted by many interconnected nanoparticles with a size of about $6{\mathrm{-}}10\ \mathrm{nm}$ . The regular inter-planar spacing of $0.244~\mathrm{{nm}}$ corresponds to the (311) lattice plane of the spinel-phase $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}}$ . The inset SAED pattern presents well-defined rings that can be assigned to spinel phase. The porosity of the $\mathrm{{Co_{3}O_{4}/C T}}$ composite has been further determined by nitrogen sorption measurement. As shown in Fig. 4(d), the as-prepared $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/C T}$ delivers a type-IV isotherm with H3-shaped hysteresis loops, with a wide hysteresis loop at the relative pressure from 0.3 to 0.9, suggesting a welldeveloped mesoporosity. The specific surface area of the sample, based on the Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) model, is calculated to $151.8\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . The pore size distribution is in the range of $1{-}12\ \mathrm{nm},$ mainly centered around $1.8\ \mathrm{nm}$ . Thus, the high specific surface area and abundant available channels ensure the $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/C T}$ to be more conducive to the electrolyte infiltration and $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ diffusion [12, 39]. The electrochemical performance of the $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/C T}$ composite is firstly evaluated through the rotating disk electrode (RDE) in the $0.1\mathrm{{M}}$ KOH solution. Although the electrolyte in the $_{\mathrm{Li-O}_{2}}$ batteries is aprotic, the catalytic performance shares the similar law in the aqueous condition. As shown in Fig. S4 in the ESM, the $\mathrm{{Co_{3}O_{4}/C T}}$ composite number of transferred electron $(n)$ is calculated to be 3.87, according to the Koutecky-Levich (K-L) equation [21, 47]. It follows a one-pot four-electron pathway and manifests a good catalytic activity for ORR.
The $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/C T}$ composites have been directly used to explore their electrochemical performance as the oxygen cathode. As shown in the Fig. 5(a), the capacity of the $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/C T}$ composite and CONS is recorded at current density of $200\ \mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ in the voltage window of $2.3\mathrm{-}4.3\mathrm{V}$ vs. $\mathrm{Li^{+}/L i}$ . The $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/C T}$ composite shows a capacity of $6{,}509\ \mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}$ , which is higher than the CONS cathode $(5,018\ \mathrm{mAh\cdotg^{-1}}^{\cdot}$ ). What’s more, the galvanostatic discharge/charge curves show that the discharge platform of the $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/C T}$ cathode is about $2.72{\mathrm{~V~}}$ and the charge potential is as low as $3.9\mathrm{V}$ . In comparison, the CONS sample delivers the charge potential of $4.12\mathrm{V}$ and the discharge potential of $2.63\mathrm{~V~}$ . The discrepancies in the charge/discharge performance have been attributed to higher charge-transfer resistance in the cathode based on CONS. The insulating polymers in the CONS increase the undesirable interparticle resistance and block the efficient contact among three-phase interface, containing catalyst, oxygen and electrolyte [39]. As a result, the AC impedance spectroscopy measurements were conducted to analyse the charge-transfer resistance in the Li- $O_{2}$ batteries. In Fig. S5 in the ESM, the EIS curves were obtained under the open circuit potential before discharge/charge process, and the corresponding equivalent circuit diagram is shown as inset graph. As shown, the diameter of the semicircle of the CONS cathode is much bigger than the $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/C T}$ cathode, indicating the higher charge-transfer resistance. The networked morphology guarantees the fast electron transport within $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanoflakes, while the binder-free design ensures the lower impedance and prevents undesired side reactions simultaneously. Moreover, cyclic voltammograms (CVs) of the $\mathrm{Li}{\cdot}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ batteries based on CONS and $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/C T}$ cathodes were also recorded to the catalytic activity with a scan rate of $0.1\mathrm{mV}{\cdot}\ s^{-1}$ (Fig. S6 in the ESM). The larger ORR current of the $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/C T}$ cathode confirms the better ORR catalytic activity. The OER process can be observed in the anodic scan, where the $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/C T}$ cathode presents lower onset potentials than the Li- $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ batteries with CONS. Both these results match well with discharge/charge capacity in Fig. 5(a).
The cycling performance of the $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/C T}$ cathode and CONS was further investigated at the current density of $200\mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ with cut-off capacity of $1{,}000\ \mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}$ . As shown in Fig. 5(b), the discharge potential of the Li- $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ batteries with $\mathrm{{Co_{3}O_{4}/C T}}$ cathode is as high as $2.75\mathrm{V}$ and the charge potential is about $3.77\mathrm{V}$ (vs. $\mathrm{Li/Li^{\cdot}}$ ). However, discharge potential of the batteries with CONS cathode is $2.66\mathrm{~V~}$ and charge potential is $4.0~\mathrm{V}$ , displaying a larger polarization for $1.34\mathrm{~V~}$ . It is noted that highly polarized potential is attributed to many undesirable side reactions, like decomposition of the carbon cathode and ethers-electrolyte [48]. What’s more, the high specific surface area and well-defined hierarchical pore structure in $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/C T}$ cathode can store sufficient electrolyte, promote the diffusion of oxygen and provide enough place for the discharge products. The $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/C T}$ cathode without binder also facilitates the electron transfer during the discharge/charge process. Thus, the $\mathrm{Li}{-}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ batteries with $\mathrm{{Co_{3}O_{4}/C T}}$ cathode contribute to a boosted cycle life for 134 times. However, the Li- $O_{2}$ batteries with CONS and binder can only cycle for 24 times, where the terminal charge potential reach to $4.7\mathrm{V}$ and the discharge platform drop to $2.5\mathrm{V}$ , as shown in Fig. S7 in the ESM. Here, the stability of TEGDME electrolyte has been tested. As shown in Fig. S8 in the ESM, the electrolyte keeps stable below $4.3\mathrm{V}$ , while the high charge potential makes the electrolyte decompose and incurs severe side reaction, leading to the death of cell after a few cycles.
To investigate the reversible formation and decomposition of discharge products on $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/C T}$ cathode, the ex-situ XRD was conducted. As shown in Fig. 6(a), the battery with $\mathrm{{Co_{3}O_{4}/C T}}$ cathode was first discharged to $2.3\mathrm{V}$ . In contrast with the pristine cathode, the discharged cathode exhibits two extra weak reflections at $33^{\circ}$ and $35^{\circ}{}_{;}$ , which are assigned to the formation of $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ [49–51]. No other by-products like $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{CO}_{3}$ and LiOH have been detected in the XRD pattern. After charged to $4.3\mathrm{V}$ , both diffraction peaks have disappeared, indicating the good reversibility of the charge/discharge process. The reversibility of $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ formation and decomposition is further directly observed via SEM images. As shown in Fig. 6(c), the toroid-like discharge products $\left(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\right)$ structure are homogeneously deposited on the cathode, where the size of $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ is about $350~\mathrm{nm}$ . After recharged, all the toroid-like lithium peroxide disappears in the Fig. 6(d) and the cathode reemerges the $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanoflakes array, indicating the high reversibility of the electrodes.
The obtained freestanding $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/C T}$ cathode shows a good flexibility with a capability of $180^{\circ}$ bending and resumes without any damage as shown in insert graph of Fig. 7(a). To further explore its potential application in flexible electronics, the $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/C T}$ cathode was assembled into pouch-type cell, with aluminum plastic as battery case, $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/C T}$ sample as oxygen cathode, LiTFSI-TEGDME as electrolyte, glass fiber as separator and Li foil as anode. The opencircuit voltage (OCV) of as-fabricated battery keeps stable, regardless of whether its working condition is flat or bent (Figs. 7(a)–7(d)). The cycling performance is further evaluated when the bending angles are $0^{\circ}$ and $180^{\circ}$ , respectively. The battery is first charged and discharged with angles of $0^{\circ}$ for 5 cycles and then bent to $180^{\circ}$ for another 5 cycles, where the curves of discharge/charge process almost remain unchanged. The results indicate that $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/C T}$ cathode has sufficient electrochemical activity and mechanical stability even under bending condition in the Li- $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ battery.
The outstanding cycling performance is mainly attributed to the following aspects and the proposed mechanism is given in Fig. 8. First of all, the spinel-phase $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}}$ shows a good high crystallization as shown in Fig. 4(b), and itself has been reported to process the bifunctional catalytic activity towards ORR and OER [29, 52]. What’s more, the $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}}$ sheets with high specific surface area are composed of nano-sized particle that ensures the sufficient contact between the active sites and oxygen. Simultaneously, the open-structure nanoflake arrays possess sufficient $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}/$ cathode contact interface and adequate $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ accommodation, leading to the larger capacity $(6,509\mathrm{\mAh{\cdotg}^{-1}})$ [12]. Secondly, the abundant hierarchical pore structure can store enough electrolyte and facilitate oxygen diffusion that enhances the effective mass transfer throughout the cathode. Thus, the $\mathrm{{Co_{3}O_{4}/C T}}$ cathode can promote the formation and accumulation of discharge products, contributing to the enhanced improvement of electrochemical performance. More importantly, compared to the CONS with binder, the morphology and pore structure of the CONS have cracked and destroyed as shown in Fig. S3 in the ESM. The organic binders are introduced to fabricate the oxygen cathode, leading to the higher impedance for electron transfer during the electrochemical reaction (Fig. S5 in the ESM) [8]. In contrast, the metallic catalysts $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}}$ directly grown on carbon textile without binder can extensively improve charge transfer between catalysts and carbon textile. The three-dimensional- networked nanoflake arrays also guarantee the fast electron transport within $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanoflakes. Consequently, the Li- $O_{2}$ battery with CONS cathode delivers high overpotential and some detrimental side reactions can cause the poor cycling stability in comparison to the $\mathrm{{Co_{3}O_{4}/C T}}$ cathode. Finally, the uniform $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanoflakes on the carbon textile can also release the volume change during the charge-discharge process. Hence, these consequences further demonstrated that the binder-free $\mathrm{{Co_{3}O_{4}/C T}}$ sample with abundant hierarchical pore structure delivers the excellent electrochemical activity and can serve as promising oxygen electrode in the Li- ${{\bf{O}}_{2}}$ batteries.
## 4 Conclusions
To summarize, the spatially-controlled $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanoflakes converted from 2D-MOFs were successfully grown on the flexible carbon textile, through a facile liquid-phase deposition method and following heat treatment. When the $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/C T}$ samples are directly used as the oxygen electrodes, the as-assembled Li- $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ batteries deliver a high specific capacity $(6,509\mathrm{\mAh{\cdotg}^{-1}},$ ) and long-term cycling stability. The improved performance of the $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/C T}$ cathode is mainly attributed to its suitable binder-free structure and tailored properties, which can not only enhance the mass transport, but also increase the electrical conductivity. A pouch-type Li- $O_{2}$ battery with $\mathrm{{Co_{3}O_{4}/C T}}$ cathode directly as oxygen electrodes shows a great cycling stability under different bending condition, demonstrating its potential application for flexible electronics and irregularly shaped energy storage devices.
